Currently, if you die in Sokoban, your game is not eligible
to leave bones.  I think I understand why the developers
made this choice, but ...

I have a proposal for how Sokoban bones could be created
without screwing things up too horribly badly.  It does
require that the game know where the level definition file
is that was used to generate the level in the first place;
(I don't happen to know if vanilla NetHack keeps track of
that for already-generated levels, but it would not be
difficult to do.)

It would also mean that any major changes to the Sokoban
level definitions would break bones compatibility for the
changed levels, but since currently there *is* no bones
compatibility for Sokoban levels, since there are no bones
for Sokoban, that doesn't seem like it makes anything worse.

Okay, so here's my proposal:

When the player dies on Sokoban, the usual probability
checks (based on dungeon level) would be made, just like on
any other level.  If they pass, so that bones are to be
saved, the following things would happen to the level, in
the following order, before it is written to a bones file:

1. All boulders currently on the level, including ones in
   monster or player inventory, are removed.  Also, any pits
   currently on the level are removed (temporarily).

2. Except for the player (who is about to be undead anyway)
   but including pets if applicable, any monster currently
   on the level that is capable of picking up boulders is
   replaced with a new random monster (subject to the usual
   Sokoban probabilities for monster selection).

3. If the dying player was standing on the burned-Elbereth
   prize location in Sokoban level 4, he is moved into one
   of the two other alcoves.  In the very unlikely even that
   there are already graves in both of those locations, then
   the player is moved out into the treasure zoo.  This
   prevents the grave site and bones pile from ever being in
   the prize spot.  If necessary, another monster can be
   displaced or eliminated to make room for the player
   gravesite.

4. The usual bones things happen (i.e., the grave is placed,
   the player is converted to a ghost or whatnot, inventory
   is subject to curses and is dumped on the loot pile,
   etc), just like when any other level is bones-ified.

5. The pits that should be on the level (originally, as per
   the level file) are restored, _except_ for any that would
   be on the grave location.  Monsters on the pit locations
   are displaced (i.e., relocated elsewhere on the level) so
   that the level is not saved with monsters at the bottom
   of pits.  If the level is _full_ (no place to put the
   monsters due to all tiles being taken), monsters can
   simply be removed.  If the grave is on a location that
   would otherwise get a pit, there will be one fewer pits.
   Potentially, if a player loads a Sokoban bones level and
   dies and multi-player bones are saved with an additional
   grave, there could be more than one pit missing.  I
   believe all the existing NetHack Sokoban levels can be
   solved with several fewer pits than usual, because most
   of the boulders are not directly in the player's way.
   (It's the pits that are in the way of reaching the goal.)

6. The boulders that should be on the level (originally, as
   per the level file) are restored, displacing any monsters
   that are in the way.  A boulder *can* be placed on the
   grave site, if the grave happens to be where the boulder
   is supposed to go.  This means that the grave and bones
   pile can potentially be under a boulder, if the player
   happened to die in a boulder's starting position.

7. If this is the fourth level of Sokoban, the prize is
   checked.  If it is not present in its designated
   location, a new one is placed there.  (This implies that
   if the player had already picked up the prize, there can
   be an extra copy of it, but it would be the SAME prize,
   because it's from the same version of the fourth level of
   Sokoban, obviously.  I do not believe that a second bag
   of holding or a second amulet of reflection would be very
   unbalancing.  An altogether missing prize, or even the
   increased probability of its being cursed if in a bones
   pile, would be a much larger change.  This avoids that,
   without a lot of complex conditional logic.)

8. If this is the fourth level of Sokoban, any empty tiles
   in the treasure zoo are repopulated with gold and
   monster(s).  This means there can be extra gold and/or
   extra monsters if some have merely moved and not been
   removed, but the potential for extra gold and extra
   monsters in a bones pile would not be unique to Sokoban
   by any means.

I *think* that covers all the major bases, for ensuring a
more-or-less reasonable Sokoban experience, while still
allowing the bones of a player who dies there to be found.

Thoughts?



